  "notStated": [
    "Where the decay and sustain times stop being measured. Both columns are internally consistent to within two per cent of one endpoint, and the document never says what level that endpoint is.",
    "The sample rate. 32 kHz falls out of the noise clock table's top row rather than being printed as a figure.",
    "Anything about cycles. The manual describes the DSP in terms of times and frequencies, so nothing here can settle when within a sample period a register is read.",
    "The gaussian interpolation kernel. The manual describes pitch as a 14 bit value and never gives the filter that resamples. It is on the die and has never been dumped as a file, so unlike every image this family loads there is no canonical artifact to hash and no digest here to confirm a copy against. A caller may supply their own through Chip(gaussian=...); check_kernel in sdsp/tables.py holds it to the length, the shape and the identity that the four taps of every position sum to 2048, which is weaker than a digest because two different tables can pass it.",
    "What a register, a counter, a latch or the noise generator holds at power on. The model scrambles all of them from a seed; see powerOn.",
    "What a reset leaves anywhere but the flag register. The model follows the implementation the corpus was recorded from; see resetLine."
  ],
  "documents": {
    "developmentManual": {
      "publisher": "Nintendo of America Inc.",
      "title": "SNES Development Manual, Book I",
      "section": "Section 3, Super NES Sound: chapter 7 Register Used, chapter 2 BRR",
      "readOn": "2026-08-21",
      "retrievedFrom": "archive.org/details/SNESDevManual, book1_text.pdf",
      "sha256": "f1405241000046abba7849376d2ec53cf85f1ad2e9950d69a7c2cf1f3ff6b791",
      "pages": 240,
      "note": "A scan with an OCR text layer. The register map and the parameter tables were read off rendered page images at 200 dots per inch, because the text layer interleaves the table columns and reorders the numbers.",
      "file": "book1_text.pdf"
    }
  },
  "authority": {
    "order": [
      "Nintendo's SNES Development Manual, for the register map and the parameter tables it prints",
      "the clock schedule, for what happens on which of the thirty two steps a sample is made of",
      "a corpus of real game configurations, for everything neither of those reaches"
    ],
    "why": "Sony published no data sheet for this part. What exists is Nintendo's manual, written for somebody programming the sound unit rather than describing the silicon: it gives the registers, the rates and the attack times, and says nothing about the order the part does its work in. The order is what decides whether a register read taken mid sample answers what the hardware answers, so it is modelled as the fixed thirty two step schedule the part runs, and that schedule rests on a corpus rather than on a page.",
    "whatIsMissing": "A manufacturer data sheet for the S-DSP. None is known to exist, so the top rung of the ladder is occupied by a manual written for a different purpose and the rung a data sheet would occupy is empty.",
    "comparedAgainst": {
      "runner": "conformance/corpus.py",
      "what": "Real game configurations, replayed sample for sample and compared against what a second implementation of the same part produces: 240 cases and 23,040 samples, none of them derived from this model."
    }
  },
  "resetLine": {
    "value": "every register cleared and the flag register set to E0; every voice released, its envelope, decoded samples and position cleared and its block offset set to the first pair; the noise generator set to 4000; the rate counter, the clock phase and the echo position set to 0; the every-other-sample toggle set to 1; every latch the schedule carries between clocks cleared",
    "verified": false,
    "source": "snes9x 1.63 apu/bapu/dsp/SPC_DSP.cpp: SPC_DSP::load, which clears the register file and everything after it in the state block, sets FLG to E0 and every voice's block offset to 1, and SPC_DSP::soft_reset_common, which sets the noise to 4000, the toggle to 1 and the echo position, phase and counter to 0. SPC_DSP::read answers from a second copy of the registers, which load fills from a fixed table, initial_regs, rather than clearing; this model does not follow that table, because nothing in that source says where it came from. That is the implementation the corpus was recorded from, so this is rung four of the ladder, not a measurement.",
